WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.250
- Mr. Bean is an absolute cult classic,

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.770
whether you're talking about the movie,

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.360
the sitcom, even the animation series.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.360
Well, today I'm gonna be
taking a look at all of them

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.060
so we can have a laugh and
learn something in the process.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:12.938
Are you ready?

00:00:12.938 --> 00:00:13.771
(squeaking)

00:00:13.771 --> 00:00:14.921
Or should I say, (grunting)?

00:00:14.921 --> 00:00:15.754
(Mr. Bean grunting)

00:00:15.754 --> 00:00:16.660
- Sir, it's urgent,

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.595
we have a ruptured kidney
and massive bleeding.

00:00:18.595 --> 00:00:19.428
(sighing)

00:00:19.428 --> 00:00:20.950
- Okay.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:22.110
- Stethoscope down,

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.420
I wanna know how they
got a ruptured kidney.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.463
Kidneys, generally just
don't self combust.

00:00:27.463 --> 00:00:28.296
(kidney exploding)

00:00:28.296 --> 00:00:30.243
So I need to know why
this kidney ruptured.

00:00:32.911 --> 00:00:35.610
- At last, Dr Bean.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.240
- Hello Dr, got a 45 year old male.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.190
Blood type B negative,

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:40.850
brought in by paramedics 10 minutes ago.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:43.350
- He's got a penetrating
injury to the left chest.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.940
- All right, no, no.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:47.773
No.

00:00:47.773 --> 00:00:49.270
My man needs to scrub in before he goes

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:50.170
to the operating room.

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:52.100
There's a very strict
way how this happens.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.913
First, you put on your surgeon's
cap, you put on your mask

00:00:53.913 --> 00:00:56.620
then you start washing your
hands and you do a very

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.359
thorough scrub process where you disinfect

00:00:58.359 --> 00:01:01.310
and make sure you're sterile
up until your elbows.

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:03.210
Once you're sterile,
you walk into the room

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.400
making sure you're not
making contact with anything.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.830
They then have the sterile
gown for you, which you

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:09.580
insert your hands through quite gently.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:10.960
Then they have their sterile gloves

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.130
and all this is a process
that must be followed

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:14.420
to a T to make sure

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.970
that you don't cross contaminate
the sterile environment.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.590
So no, they don't just
dress you and you run

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:20.240
into the operating room, like, all right,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.210
I'm gonna save the world.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.068
(machine beeping)

00:01:24.068 --> 00:01:25.990
(laughing)

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:28.280
Now I know where that meme
comes from, number one.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.500
Number two, you don't touch your mask

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:31.520
when you're in the OR.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.460
That's not part of the sterile field.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.720
If you touch it, you
then have to disinfect,

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.570
throw everything off and
restart the whole process again.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.873
Not sterile M&amp;Ms.

00:01:46.778 --> 00:01:49.111
(laughing)

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:54.943
What is it?

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:58.103
What was that?

00:01:58.103 --> 00:01:59.361
- [Sam] I think it's a bullet.

00:01:59.361 --> 00:02:03.640
- Oh.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:04.683
Oh, don't eat it.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:10.710
Oh no!

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:12.110
Because that is an M&amp;M

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:14.640
and M&amp;M is made of chocolate
and the body is warm.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.410
When it fell into the body cavity,

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.340
it would automatically melt a little bit

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.260
of the sugar off and that
sugar will then remain

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:23.150
in the body and serve as a
breeding ground for bacteria

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:25.860
and sepsis for the patient
once they close them up.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.040
So that is actually a
really, really big problem.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:31.920
You should be irrigating that
with some saline water, stat.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:32.955
- It's crashing on us.

00:02:32.955 --> 00:02:34.750
- What's his pulse, 38?

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:35.650
That's no bueno.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:36.880
That means he's crashing.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.690
- His heart rate is dropping,
we're gonna lose him.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.360
- The suture car behind Mr.
Bean there is quite accurate.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.460
We do have a lot of
different types of suture

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:44.770
for varying utilities

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:46.953
depending on what kind of
procedure you're doing.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:49.840
- Sorry, sir, we're losing him.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.290
I've got to get in there now.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.570
Dr, you can't just--

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:58.520
It's too dangerous!

00:02:59.518 --> 00:03:01.349
(laughing)

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:04.182
(machine beeping)

00:03:05.771 --> 00:03:08.289
(metal clinging)

00:03:08.289 --> 00:03:09.289
Incredible.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:11.850
- By removing the bullet,

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:13.920
you did not save the patient's life.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.307
Imagine, someone got shot, it
tore a hole in their arteries

00:03:18.307 --> 00:03:20.030
and their organs.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:21.980
And by taking out that bullet,

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:24.280
you have not solved
the problem of the fact

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:25.980
that the patient is bleeding internally.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:29.000
So no, Mr. Bean didn't
just save him by going.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.270
- Any kind of damage

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.610
to the brain is a very
sensitive thing to observe.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.410
So maybe we should just step outside

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.160
and give the doctor a little room to work.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.530
- The first nurse said we
shouldn't think of it as a coma

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:40.160
but more like a deep sleep.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:41.840
- [Nurse] Yeah, that's sort of true.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:43.430
- Jennifer.

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:44.510
- He talks.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:47.889
- Wakey-wakey, breakfast's ready.

00:03:47.889 --> 00:03:48.722
Jennifer.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:51.323
Ba!

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.493
- Still in his bloody scrubs.

00:03:59.604 --> 00:04:00.831
- Wake up.

00:04:00.831 --> 00:04:01.985
Wake up.

00:04:01.985 --> 00:04:03.490
Wake up!

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:06.060
- Defibrillators are not
there for you to play with

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:07.970
or to wake patients up.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.730
They're meant literally
to restart your heart.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.270
And when I say restart, it doesn't mean

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:15.030
that if your heart is flat-lining,
these help it restart.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.130
What it does is it
actually stops the heart

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.540
and allows it to restart on its own.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:20.373
So it's almost

00:04:20.373 --> 00:04:22.220
like interrupting the
natural rhythm of the heart.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.490
The reason we do the defibrillation is

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:27.610
in certain instances, if a
patient has an irregular rhythm

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:29.590
to the point where it's a useless rhythm,

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:32.200
or it's going too fast,
we can then control it

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.230
by doing one of these
very controlled shocks,

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:36.360
depending on the rhythm
that we see on the monitor.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:40.330
And Mr. Bean do not use a
defibrillator to wake people up.

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:43.330
(light tense music)

00:04:47.352 --> 00:04:49.602
(buzzing)

00:04:51.543 --> 00:04:53.876
(screaming)

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.633
He didn't say clear.

00:04:56.633 --> 00:04:59.216
(light music)

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:03.090
What is up with that doctor's feet?

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:04.290
Is he a genie?

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:06.030
And I don't know what that
is in the foot of his bed.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:06.960
If that's his EKG

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:10.120
my man's having a heart
attack and we need to call--

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:11.030
We need to call somebody.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:12.090
We need to call a cardiologist.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:13.695
'Cause that's not normal.

00:05:13.695 --> 00:05:15.853
- [Nurse] Let's take your temperature.

00:05:16.766 --> 00:05:17.599
In we go.

00:05:23.333 --> 00:05:25.033
- He's gonna give himself a fever.

00:05:26.223 --> 00:05:27.806
- Right, you ready?

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:29.633
Oh!

00:05:29.633 --> 00:05:31.480
- This is malingering at its finest,

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:32.940
where he's trying to pretend he's ill

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:34.510
in order to get care from somebody else.

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:36.610
There's actually individuals where there's

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:38.860
Munchausen syndrome by proxy.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:40.040
When you have an individual

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:42.090
who is keeping their family members sick

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:43.450
so they're reliant on them.

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:45.270
I'm gonna link some resources down below

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:47.502
just 'cause I think it's
a really interesting read.

00:05:47.502 --> 00:05:49.501
(upbeat music)

00:05:49.501 --> 00:05:51.515
(screaming)

00:05:51.515 --> 00:05:53.280
(sighing)

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:54.113
I don't understand.

00:05:54.113 --> 00:05:54.970
She checked his temperature.

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:55.803
He had a fever.

00:05:55.803 --> 00:05:57.700
She's rushing him to x-ray.

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:00.140
Does you want to check
if he has a pneumonia?

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:02.503
Maybe you want to listen
to his lungs first.

00:06:02.503 --> 00:06:05.070
(spooky music)

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.660
- Right.

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:07.493
- Oh no.

00:06:07.493 --> 00:06:09.580
Is he gonna get an x-ray of his chest

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:11.480
that they're gonna see the teddy bear?

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.350
First of all, a teddy bear
wouldn't show up like that.

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:17.640
Unless the Teddy bear was
made out of like metal

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.759
but otherwise you wouldn't just
see an outline of the bear.

00:06:20.759 --> 00:06:23.592
(machine beeping)

00:06:27.094 --> 00:06:28.584
- Scalpel.

00:06:28.584 --> 00:06:29.417
- Scalpel.

00:06:29.417 --> 00:06:31.390
- How about putting him to sleep?

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:34.380
Get a little anesthesiology
action in there.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:37.496
No, we don't sharpen our scalpels
like we're going cooking.

00:06:37.496 --> 00:06:39.017
(gasping)

00:06:39.017 --> 00:06:41.580
(screaming)

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.290
Look at the signs in the background here:

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:44.560
poorly, to the right.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:45.720
Healthy to the left,

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:47.880
cured to the right,
restaurant to the right.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.280
What is a poorly?

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:50.347
- Come back here!

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:56.140
Oh goodness me.

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:59.840
- Again signs, restaurant,
chapel, bar, exit,

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:00.770
down, dead.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:03.640
First of all, what hospital has a bar?

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:05.590
Actually, I think a
Grey's Anatomy hospital

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:06.423
might have a bar.

00:07:06.423 --> 00:07:08.550
I remember them like in the first episode,

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:11.540
right in between of doing a
procedure and being post-call,

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:13.500
they were like, let's just
go grab a drink at the bar.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:14.990
And then they all come
back to the hospital

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:15.823
like 10 minutes later.

00:07:15.823 --> 00:07:16.920
Where is that bar?

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:18.270
Maybe it's in the hospital.

00:07:21.313 --> 00:07:23.629
(gasping)

00:07:23.629 --> 00:07:26.712
(wheels screeching)

00:07:27.703 --> 00:07:30.380
There's gonna be some
serious burns on his hand.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:33.440
(screaming)

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:35.220
There's definitely an ICD-10 code.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:37.950
The ICD-10 is the
International Classification

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:39.450
of Diseases Manual 10.

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:42.840
And it's gotten so specific
that if I have a patient come in

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.540
as a result of a fall, I
can't just type in fall.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:46.520
I mean I can,

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:48.610
but it's gonna give me
all these sub definitions

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:51.130
of like fall on concrete, fall on curb,

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:54.720
fall in park, fall in gym,
fall on grass, recently mode.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:55.770
I'm not even joking.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:57.100
I'm gonna try and find some weird ones

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:58.350
to pop up on screen here.

00:08:00.147 --> 00:08:03.498
(sirens waling)

00:08:03.498 --> 00:08:06.581
(audience laughing)

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:13.590
There's a good reason why all
ambulances have a giant sign

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:17.260
on the back of them that say,
keep at least 50 yards back

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:19.200
so they could actually
open their back door

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:21.750
and allow the patient or help the patient,

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:23.700
escort the patient into the hospital.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:26.840
Because a lot of times
they're on a mobile bed.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:28.210
In order for that bed to come out

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:30.945
you can't have a car
parked directly behind it.

00:08:30.945 --> 00:08:34.028
(audience laughing)

00:08:35.813 --> 00:08:38.043
Why does he have a tea kettle?

00:08:39.014 --> 00:08:42.820
(audience laughing)

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:44.293
Oh no, don't take her ticket.

00:08:56.230 --> 00:08:57.063
That's so evil.

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:05.390
That's worse than licking
the hospital floor.

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:07.980
The dirtiest part of your
body are your fingers.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:10.080
And everyone's touching that thing.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:12.070
Here's another 90s, funny sit-com,

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:14.130
me reviewing medical scenes from Seinfeld.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:17.000
And here's the truth about
cracking your knuckles.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:17.833
So I just did it,

00:09:17.833 --> 00:09:19.830
but click here to find
out what that truth is

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:21.803
and as always stay happy and healthy.

00:09:22.757 --> 00:09:25.924
(light upbeat music)

